-- title:
--   Chart algebra at a transcendental j is finite over R[j]
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a discrete valuation ring (a commutative domain with the `IsDiscreteValuationRing` property), let $K_0$ be a field which is a fraction field of $R$, and let $F$ be a field carrying compatible $R$- and $K_0$-algebra structures (so that $R \to K_0 \to F$ is a scalar tower). Let $j \in F$ be a nonzero element which is transcendental over $K_0$, and assume that $F$ is a finite-dimensional and separable extension of the intermediate field $K_0(j) =$ `IntermediateField.adjoin K₀ {j}`. Write `chartAlgFin R F j` for the $R$-subalgebra of $F$ consisting of all elements of $F$ that are integral over the $R$-subalgebra $R[j] =$ `Algebra.adjoin R {j}`, and `jChartFin R F j` for the element $j$ regarded as a member of that subalgebra. The assertion is that `chartAlgFin R F j` is a finite module over the $R$-subalgebra of `chartAlgFin R F j` generated by `jChartFin R F j`, i.e. over the copy of $R[j]$ sitting inside the chart algebra.
--
--   This is the finiteness statement for the affine chart of the two-chart integral model of a curve over $R$ with coordinate $j$: the integral closure of $R[j]$ in $F$ is a finite $R[j]$-module, in the form (finiteness over the internally generated copy of $R[j]$) used downstream. It is invoked in the construction and analysis of integral models of modular curves at full level, in particular in the statements about blow-up charts, Drinfeld chart witnesses and function-field points on the Tate-divisor side.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicCurve_TwoChartIntegralModel_moduleFinite_adjoin_jChartFin_chartAlgFin.lean

